[38;2;124;124;124mA simple coalgebra over an algebraically closed field $\mathbb{K}$ is of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mform $M_n(\mathbb{K})^*$, i.e., the dual of a matrix algebra. For it to be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcocommutative... Actually, cocommutativity is a property of the coalgebra [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstructure. A simple coalgebra $C$ is isomorphic to $M_n(\mathbb{K})^*$ as a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcoalgebra. The coalgebra $M_n(\mathbb{K})^*$ is cocommutative if and only if $n [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 1$. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWhy? The coalgebra $M_n(\mathbb{K})^*$ has comultiplication dual to the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmultiplication of $M_n(\mathbb{K})$. For cocommutativity, we need $\Delta = \tau[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\circ \Delta$. This corresponds to the multiplication of $M_n(\mathbb{K})$ being[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcommutative, which happens iff $n = 1$ (since $M_n(\mathbb{K})$ is commutative [0m
[38;2;124;124;124miff $n = 1$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo a cocommutative coalgebra over an algebraically closed field has coradical [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat is a direct sum of 1-dimensional simple subcoalgebras, i.e., it is pointed.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo cocommutative implies pointed over algebraically closed fields. Good, so the [0m[38;2;124;124;124m"pointed" hypothesis is redundant given cocommutativity over an algebraically [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclosed field.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the structure theorem gives $A \cong U(\mathfrak{g}) \# \mathbb{K}G$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is: when is this cosemisimple?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCosemisimple means $A$ is a direct sum of simple subcoalgebras as a coalgebra [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(i.e., the coalgebra is cosemisimple, meaning every comodule is completely [0m
